course: dictionary
lesson: 5483
title: Lately and recent time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lately is an adverb. It tells you when something happens: in the time just before now. «What have you been up to lately?» asks about the last few days or weeks, not one single moment.
explain: Lately often goes with have been or has been, because those words show something that has gone on for a while. «I have been tired lately» means I have felt tired for days or weeks, not just once.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In «Lately boys from both streets had started looking at Maddie oddly», the word started shows a change that began not long ago. In «lately it's always Lily», the word always shows a pattern that keeps happening. Lately is the signal that this is recent.
fact: Lately means recently, in the time just before now.
fact: Lately is an adverb, so it tells you about time, and it often goes with «have been» or «has been».
fact: Lately suggests a pattern or a change that is new, not one single event.
fact: Phrases with a similar meaning are «just lately» and «of late», and «of late» is more formal.
fact: An old-fashioned meaning is «not long ago», as in «lately of London», which means formerly living in London.
exercise: «What have you been up to lately?» What does lately mean here? | Very quickly, in a rush | In the future | Many years ago, long before now | *Recently, over the last few days or weeks
exercise: «Lately it's always Lily, staring at two heads whispering and giggling.» Which word shows a pattern that keeps going? | Lily | staring | *always | giggling
exercise: Mum asks, «Have you seen your cousins lately?» What is she asking? | Did you see them a long time ago, when you were small? | Will you see them tomorrow? | *Have you seen them in the last few weeks? | Did you see them in a hurry?
test: Which phrase means the same as lately? | Long ago | Tomorrow | *Just lately | Carefully
test: Sam says, «I have been tired lately.» What does this tell the reader? | Sam is going to be tired tomorrow | Sam was tired once, a long time ago | *Sam has felt tired for the last few days or weeks | Sam is always tired, since he was born
exam: Which sentence uses lately correctly to show something recent? | Lately, the dinosaurs will return next week. | Lately she ate a carrot for lunch. | She was born lately, in the year 1990. | *She has been very busy lately.
